drills.explanation:
Compare ids only when both are assigned, and keep the hash constant:

```java
@Override public boolean equals(Object o) {
    if (this == o) return true;
    if (!(o instanceof Order other)) return false;
    Long mine = getId(), theirs = other.getId();
    return mine != null && mine.equals(theirs);
}
@Override public int hashCode() { return Order.class.hashCode(); }
```

The tests catch the IDE-generated version, `Objects.equals(id, other.id)` with `Objects.hash(id)`: the hash moves when the id is assigned (the set loses the entity) and two unsaved entities compare equal because null equals null (the boundary test). A version with `getClass() != o.getClass()` or a direct `other.id` field read fails the proxy test. A constant hash puts every order in one bucket, which is fine for the small sets entities live in; with a stable natural key (an order number) you can hash that instead.

