drills.explanation:
Persist manages the argument; merge copies onto the managed instance and returns it:

```java
if (entity.id == null) {
    entity.id = nextId++;
    managed.put(entity.id, entity);
    return entity;
}
var target = find(entity.id);           // identity map first, else one select
if (target != entity) target.status = entity.status;
return target;
```

The tests catch the plausible wrong version that puts the detached argument into the persistence context (or returns it) on merge: then the argument's later edit is flushed, the result is the argument itself, and an already managed instance would be replaced by a second object for the same id. That is the opposite of JPA's contract, and the reason `save`'s return value must be used.
